**VERDICT: No additional bits/bytes sibling confirmed. I found three unrelated dead guards, one always-true test, and a separate ARM shift-count defect.**

I screened **224 C/header files**: 122 under `devices/`, 102 under `cpus/`, including 50 generated files. I traced **60 shortlisted expression sites across 18 files**, including your supplied instance. This was static source analysis; all reading stayed under `GXEMUL-SEC/src/`, and nothing was changed.

1. **Three provably dead guards — `dec21143_tx()`**

   In [dev_dec21143.c](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_dec21143.c:703):

   - `bufsize < 0` at lines **752 and 784**
   - `bufsize > 65536` at line **754**

   The defining assignments are:

   ```c
   buf1_size = tdes1 & TDCTL_SIZE1;
   buf2_size = (tdes1 & TDCTL_SIZE2) >> TDCTL_SIZE2_SHIFT;
   bufsize = buf1_size? buf1_size : buf2_size;
   ```

   [tulipreg.h](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/thirdparty/tulipreg.h:228) defines the masks as `0x000007ff` and `0x003ff800`, with shift `11`. Consequently, both sizes range over **0–2047 bytes**; selecting either gives **`bufsize ∈ {0,…,2047}`**. No intervening assignment expands that range on either relevant path.

   All three predicates are therefore **always false**. Their clamp assignments have no current behavioral effect. The separate comparison `bufsize > 65536 - d->cur_tx_buf_len` at line 786 is **not** established dead: it involves the accumulated length.

2. **Always-true test — `dec21143_rx()`**

   The same file initializes [`writeback_len = 4`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_dec21143.c:353), with no subsequent assignment. Its reachable set is therefore **`{4}`**.

   Exact expression: **`writeback_len > 1`**, line **468**.

   This is **always true**, so its body executes. The variable counts descriptor words; the transfer length is explicitly `sizeof(uint32_t) * writeback_len`. That conversion is consistent. This is a redundant test, with no demonstrated format-selection error.

3. **Separate observable correctness issue — ARM bit-field extraction**

   In [cpu_arm_instr.c](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:1309), both **`arm_instr_ubfx()`** and **`arm_instr_sbfx()`** evaluate:

   ```c
   uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
   ```

   Locations: **1316 and 1340**.

   The [decoder](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:4481) defines `width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)`, packs it into `arg[2]`, and each handler recovers it with `arg[2] >> 16`. Thus **`width ∈ {1,…,32}` bits**.

   On a host with 32-bit `int`, the decoded value 32 permits **`1 << 32`**, which has undefined behavior. The `width < 32` comparison in `sbfx()` occurs **after** mask construction. The extracted register value depends on that mask; I did not measure a particular build’s resulting behavior.

The closest unit-confusion candidates cleared were:

| File/function and exact expression | Derived values and result |
|---|---|
| [dev_sgi_gbe.c](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_gbe.c:171), `dev_sgi_gbe_tick()`: `bytes_per_pixel == 1`, `bytes_per_pixel == 2` | `bitdepth = 8 << (field & 3)` gives `{8,16,32,64}` bits; `/ 8` gives **`{1,2,4,8}` bytes**. The overlay assignment adds only `1`. Both tests are reachable. |
| [dev_px.c](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_px.c:145), `dev_px_dma()`: `bytesperpixel == 3`, lines 410 and 510 | Initialization assigns depth `24`, with two device-type cases assigning `8`; there are no other depth assignments. `bitdepth >> 3` gives **`{1,3}` bytes**. Both format-selection branches are reachable. |

The survey supports **no additional confirmed unit-confusion case**, with the unrelated findings above.
